-- title:
--   A Gauss valuation subring of the q-expansion function field at p
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $\Gamma\le\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$. Write $F=$ `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ Γ` for the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ obtained by adjoining to $\mathbb{Q}$ all quotients $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\mathbb{Q}\,p_f/\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\mathbb{Q}\,p_g$, where $f,g$ are modular forms of some weight $k$ for the image of $\Gamma$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g\in\mathbb{Z}[[q]]$ and $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\mathbb{Q}\,p_g\neq 0$, and let $R=$ [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt p`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) be the subring of $\mathbb{Q}$ of rationals whose denominator is coprime to $p$, with reduction map $\rho=$ [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAtResidue p`](def/GaloisRep_RatLocalizedAtResidue.html#L15) $:R\to\mathbb{Z}/p$. Assume $j(q)=$ `jqModC ℚ` $=q^{-1}\cdot\big(E_4^3\,\eta^{-24}\big)$ lies in the level-one field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, and let $j_\Gamma=$ `jAt Γ hj` be the corresponding element of $F$. Then there is a valuation subring $W_0$ of $F$ such that: (i) $f\in W_0$ if and only if there are $a,a'\in R[[q]]$ with $\rho(a')\neq 0$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/p)[[q]]$ and $f\cdot a'=a$ in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, the power series being viewed in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ through their coefficient maps to $\mathbb{Q}$; (ii) the image of $R$ in $F$ lies in $W_0$; (iii) the image of the ideal $pR$ consists of non-units of $W_0$; (iv) for every $Q\in R[T]$ with $\rho(Q)\neq 0$, both $Q(j_\Gamma)$ and $Q(j_\Gamma)^{-1}$ lie in $W_0$; (v) for every non-unit $x$ of $W_0$, $x\cdot p^{-1}\in W_0$.
--
--   This constructs the Gauss (sup-norm) extension to $F$ of the $p$-adic valuation of $\mathbb{Q}$: by (i) it is cut out by ratios of $p$-integral $q$-expansions, by (iv) it lies above the generic point of the $j$-line modulo $p$, and by (iii), (v) its maximal ideal is generated by $p$, so the associated valuation is unramified over $\mathbb{Q}_p$ with value group $\mathbb{Z}$. It serves as the local ring at the generic point of the component of the special fibre through the cusp $\infty$, and is used downstream in the comparison of this valuation with Atkin–Lehner and diamond operators, in chart computations for $q$-expansions of $j$, and in regularity statements for $q$-expansions of cusp forms.
